      "inputTokens": 163,
      "outputTokens": 1000,
      "costUsd": 0.0090815,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here: the region between y equals the natural log of x, the y-axis, and the lines y equals zero and y equals one. It's the shape of the water in a pool with a sloping beach entry. The deep end's wall is the y-axis, the floor is flat at y equals zero, and then it rises along the log curve to the water's edge. Here's the catch. In this course, we have no rule to integrate the natural log of x. So we can't stand strips up on the x-axis under this curve, and add them up. But water settles in flat layers, so the strips can lie sideways instead. So how do we find areas using y, and how can a reflected exponential graph help?"
    },
